\DOC ACCUMULATEX_ARITH_TAC

\TYPE {ACCUMULATEX_ARITH_TAC : term list -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Rewrites the assumptions about a named machine state so that a raw
word-arithmetic expression for a just-computed register/flag value is replaced
by a fresh abbreviated result variable together with an arithmetic equation
characterising it.

\DESCRIBE
{ACCUMULATEX_ARITH_TAC excls s} takes {excls : term list}, a list of terms
excluded from the rewriting/abbreviation (in the observed traces either empty
or a supplied list), and {s : string}, the name of the state whose assumptions
are processed; {s} also fixes the suffix of the newly introduced variables. On
success it leaves exactly one goal with the same conclusion. It deletes one or
two assumptions of the form {read ... s = t} (and/or a flag assumption
expressed as a comparison on {val}-terms), where {t} is a composite
word/arithmetic expression, and puts in their place assumptions equating the
same locations to fresh variables named after {s}, together with a new
assumption giving the exact arithmetic identity defining those variables (an
equation over the reals of the {2 pow 64 * bitval ... + val ... = ...} style).
All other assumptions are left untouched.

\FAILURE
In the observed traces it fails with a {tryfind} error when no assumption about
the named state matches the pattern the tactic looks for, i.e. the state's
assumptions contain no composite word-arithmetic result expression that it can
abbreviate; this happens both with an empty and with a non-empty exclusion
list.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of rewriting, on goals whose
conclusion is an {eventually}-style machine-semantics statement and whose
assumption list contains an assumption naming the state {s}.

\ENDDOC
